I've made 943 serious mistakes in my life. I'm documenting every one of them so you don't have to make the same ones. My name is Lou Zerr. Here's another 8 of 943 (part 1 of 7).\par\par\par
The Tweet That Cost Me $300\par\par\par
It was 11:47pm on a Sunday and I was about to hand $300 to a man with an anime profile picture.\par\par\par
It was 11:47pm on a Sunday and I was about to hand $300 to a man with an anime profile picture.\par\par\par
I didn't know it yet. That's the thing about handing money to strangers. You never know you're doing it. You think you're making a decision.\par\par\par
Let me set the scene. My wife was in the living room watching some British baking show where they cry over undercooked tarts. I was in the kitchen, laptop open, one leg tucked under me on the chair because I'd read somewhere that sitting like that was bad for your hip and I liked doing it anyway. Small rebellion. The kind you have at 55.\par\par\par
I had a Kalshi account. If you don't know Kalshi, don't learn. It's a website where you bet real dollars on whether things will happen. Elections. Fed rates. Whether some hurricane will make landfall in a specific county by Tuesday. They call the bets 'contracts.' The word 'contract' makes it sound legitimate. Like you're at a desk. Like there's a lawyer somewhere.\par\par\par
There is not a lawyer somewhere.\par\par\par
I had $312 in the account. I know because I remember the number exactly. I had put in $500 four months earlier and I was up $9 for September. Nine dollars. I want to say that number again because it matters. Nine dollars. I was proud of the nine dollars in the way a man is proud of nothing else in his life so he settles on nine dollars from a gambling website.\par\par\par
I was scrolling Twitter. Not looking for anything. That's how it always starts. You're not looking. You're just scrolling. Waiting for your brain to give up so you can sleep.\par\par\par
And then I saw it.\par\par\par
'Kalshi is mispriced on Sunday. If you know, you know.'\par\par\par
Sixty-two characters. Anime avatar. Handle was something like EdgeLord_Alpha, I don't remember exactly, and I'm not going back to look because I don't want to give him the click. He had 4,200 followers. A blue check. Some bio about being a 'sharp' who runs a 'model.' No mention of what the model models. No screenshots of returns. Nothing.\par\par\par
I stared at that tweet for maybe two minutes.\par\par\par
Here's what I want you to understand. I have a business degree. I ran a small operation for eleven years. I have read approximately four books about behavioral finance. I know, in the way that you know your name, that anonymous Twitter accounts telling you a market is mispriced are lying or wrong or both.\par\par\par
I knew it.\par\par\par
And I clicked over to Kalshi anyway.\par\par\par
The market he was talking about \emdash  I figured out which one from context in the replies \emdash  was a Sunday-close contract on some economic indicator I'd never traded before. I didn't understand the underlying. I didn't understand why it might be mispriced. I saw the 'yes' side was at 42 cents. I saw a lot of volume. I saw people in the replies saying 'in' with fire emojis.\par\par\par
I put in $300.\par\par\par
I did not stop to check my logic because I did not have logic. I had a tweet. I had a stranger. I had the word 'sharp' rattling around in my brain like a coin in a dryer.\par\par\par
I hit confirm. My hand was steady. That's what I remember most. My hand was steady because I had already decided this wasn't my decision. It was EdgeLord's. If it worked, I was smart. If it didn't, he was wrong. Beautiful little accounting trick your brain does when you're too tired to be a person.\par\par\par
I closed the laptop. I brushed my teeth. My wife asked what I was up to. I said 'nothing.' First lie of the night. Not the last.\par\par\par
I went to bed thinking maybe, just maybe, I had finally figured something out.\par\par\par
Tomorrow I'll tell you what I woke up to.\par\par\par
Tomorrow: the morning after, and the number in red.\par\par\par
\emdash  Lou\par\par\par
